EXAMPLES = [
("why is the sky blue?",
"""Sunlight looks white, but it is a mixture of every color, and on its way down it keeps colliding with molecules of nitrogen and oxygen far smaller than its own wavelengths. Those molecules scatter short wavelengths much more readily than long ones, so blue light gets thrown around the whole sky while red and yellow mostly pass straight through. Wherever you look overhead, you're seeing blue light that was knocked toward you from somewhere else. At sunset the light reaches you through far more air, the blue has been scattered away long before it arrives, and what remains is the orange the day ends on."""),

("how do airplanes actually stay up",
"""A wing is shaped and tilted so that it pushes air downward as it moves forward. Push air down and the air pushes back up; that reaction is lift. The curve of the wing helps by bending the airflow smoothly, so the air above it speeds up and its pressure drops while the air beneath stays slower and presses harder. As long as the engines keep the plane moving fast enough, the wing keeps deflecting enough air to hold up the weight of everything on board. This is why speed matters so much at takeoff: the wing only works once there is enough air rushing past it to work with."""),

("why do we dream",
"""Nobody knows for certain, which is one of the more charming facts in science. The leading ideas are not mutually exclusive. Dreams may be a byproduct of the brain consolidating memories during sleep, replaying and reshuffling the day's experiences as it decides what to keep. They may help us process emotion, since the regions that handle feeling are busy during dreaming while the ones that handle logic go quiet, which would explain why dreams are vivid and absurd at once. Or they may be the sleeping brain's attempt to make a story out of random internal activity. Most likely, dreaming does several of these things at once, and the experience we wake up remembering is the narrative the brain stitched over the work."""),

("what causes the seasons",
"""The Earth's axis is tilted about 23 degrees, and it stays pointed the same way as we travel around the sun. For half the year the Northern Hemisphere leans toward the sun: sunlight strikes it more directly and the days run long, so it warms into summer. Six months later it leans away, the light arrives at a shallow angle and spreads thin, the days shorten, and winter sets in, while the Southern Hemisphere enjoys its summer. Distance has almost nothing to do with it. The Earth is actually closest to the sun in early January, in the middle of the northern winter."""),

("how does a vaccine work?",
"""Your immune system learns by experience. The first time it meets a new germ, it needs days to work out which antibodies and cells can recognize it, and in the meantime you get sick. A vaccine offers that education without the illness. It shows your body a harmless version of the germ's distinctive parts: a weakened or inactivated virus, a single protein, or instructions for making that protein. Your immune system studies it, mounts a response, and then keeps memory cells on hand. If the real germ ever arrives, those cells recognize it at once and respond within hours instead of days, usually before the infection can take hold."""),

("why is the ocean salty but rivers aren't",
"""Rivers do carry salt, just in amounts too small to taste. Rain is slightly acidic, and as it runs over rock it dissolves tiny quantities of minerals, sodium and chloride among them, and rivers deliver those to the sea. When seawater evaporates, the water rises but the salt stays behind. Repeat that for a few billion years and the dissolved minerals accumulate until roughly three and a half percent of seawater, by weight, is salt. A river is a conveyor belt; the ocean is where the conveyor ends."""),

("how do bees make honey",
"""A forager bee drinks nectar from flowers and stores it in a second stomach, where enzymes begin breaking its sugars into simpler ones. Back at the hive, she passes the nectar to house bees, mouth to mouth, and each exchange adds more enzymes. The bees then deposit it into wax cells and fan it with their wings until most of the water evaporates and the thin, watery nectar thickens into honey. When it's thick enough to resist spoiling, they seal each cell with a cap of wax. A single bee makes only about a twelfth of a teaspoon in her life, so every jar is the work of a very large crowd."""),

("what is a black hole, in simple terms",
"""A black hole is a place where so much mass has been crushed into so small a space that its gravity becomes inescapable. Every object has an escape velocity, the speed you'd need to break free of its pull; for Earth it's about eleven kilometers per second. Squeeze enough mass tightly enough and that escape velocity exceeds the speed of light. Since nothing travels faster than light, nothing that crosses the boundary, called the event horizon, ever comes back out. Most black holes form when very massive stars run out of fuel and collapse under their own weight."""),

("why do cats purr",
"""Cats purr by rapidly twitching the muscles of the larynx, which makes the vocal cords separate and snap together as the cat breathes in and out, roughly twenty to thirty times a second. The interesting part is why. Contentment is the familiar reason, but cats also purr when they're frightened, injured, or giving birth, which suggests purring is less an announcement of happiness than a way of soothing themselves or signaling that they mean no harm. Kittens purr while nursing, and mother cats purr back, so the habit may begin as a quiet conversation between them."""),

("how does a fridge keep stuff cold",
"""A refrigerator doesn't make cold; it moves heat out of the box and dumps it into your kitchen. It does this with a fluid that boils at a very low temperature. Inside the fridge, the fluid evaporates in a set of coils, and evaporation absorbs heat, the same reason a wet arm feels cool in a breeze. A compressor then squeezes the warm vapor, raising its temperature, and pumps it to coils on the back or underside, where it releases that heat into the room and condenses back into a liquid. The liquid returns inside to evaporate again, and the cycle repeats. If you've ever felt warm air near the bottom of a fridge, you were feeling the heat it had carried out of your groceries."""),

("why do leaves change color in the fall",
"""The colors were mostly there all along. Through the summer, leaves are packed with chlorophyll, the green pigment that captures sunlight, and it is so abundant that it hides the yellow and orange pigments underneath. As days shorten and nights cool, the tree stops making chlorophyll and begins reabsorbing what's left, and the hidden yellows emerge. The reds and purples are different: some trees actively produce those pigments in autumn, especially after sunny days and cool nights, which is why a bright, crisp fall produces the most vivid color."""),

("what's the difference between weather and climate",
"""Weather is what's happening in the sky today: whether it's raining, how hot it is, which way the wind blows. Climate is the pattern those days add up to over decades, the typical temperatures and rainfall of a place and how much they tend to vary. A useful way to hold the difference: weather is your mood, climate is your personality. One cold week says very little about climate, just as one bad afternoon says little about who you are."""),

("how do noise cancelling headphones work",
"""Sound is a wave of pressure, alternating crests and troughs. Noise-cancelling headphones carry small microphones that listen to the sound around you, and a chip inside calculates its exact opposite: a wave with crests wherever the noise has troughs. The speakers play that inverted wave along with your music, and when the two meet in your ear, they cancel out. It works best on steady, low sounds like engine drone or air conditioning, which are predictable enough to counter in real time. Sudden, sharp noises like a voice or a clatter change too quickly, which is why you can still hear someone call your name."""),

("why does bread rise",
"""Yeast is a living fungus, and when you mix it into dough it begins eating the sugars in the flour. As it feeds, it releases carbon dioxide. On its own, that gas would simply escape, but kneading develops gluten, a stretchy network of proteins that traps the gas in thousands of small bubbles. The dough swells like a sponge being slowly inflated. In the oven, the heat makes the trapped gas expand one last time before the structure sets, and the bubbles become the airy holes you see when you slice the loaf."""),

("how do birds know where to migrate",
"""They use several tools at once, which is part of what makes migration so remarkable. Many birds navigate by the sun during the day and by the rotation of the stars at night. Many can also sense the Earth's magnetic field, which gives them something like a built-in compass. Landmarks matter too, such as coastlines, rivers, and mountain ranges, and some species seem to use smell. Much of the basic route is inherited: young birds of some species migrate successfully on their first journey, alone, to places they have never seen."""),

("is glass actually a liquid?",
"""No, though it's a durable myth. Glass is what's called an amorphous solid: its molecules are arranged in a jumble, like a liquid's, but they're locked in place. The story usually cites old cathedral windows that are thicker at the bottom, supposedly from centuries of slow flow. The real explanation is that medieval glassmakers couldn't make panes of even thickness, and glaziers tended to set the heavier edge at the bottom. Calculations suggest that glass at room temperature would take far longer than the age of the universe to flow noticeably."""),

("why do we get goosebumps",
"""Goosebumps are a leftover from a hairier past. Each hair on your body has a tiny muscle at its base, and when you're cold or frightened, your nervous system tightens those muscles and the hairs stand up, raising a little bump around each one. For our furry ancestors this was useful: raised fur traps a layer of warm air, and it makes an animal look bigger to a threat, which is exactly what a cat does when it puffs up. We've lost most of the fur but kept the reflex, so now it mostly announces cold air, or a piece of music that moves us."""),

("how far away is the moon",
"""On average, about 384,000 kilometers, or roughly 239,000 miles. The distance varies by several percent over each month because the moon's orbit is slightly oval. To put it in more human terms: you could line up all the other planets of the solar system side by side in the gap between the Earth and the moon, and the light reflecting off its surface takes a little over a second to reach your eyes."""),

("how do octopuses change color",
"""An octopus's skin is studded with thousands of tiny elastic sacs of pigment called chromatophores, each ringed with muscles wired directly to its nervous system. When the muscles pull, a sac stretches open and its color spreads across the skin; when they relax, it shrinks back to a speck. Beneath those sacs lie layers of reflective cells that bounce light around and can produce whites and shimmering blues. Because it's all under direct nervous control, an octopus can change its appearance in a fraction of a second, and even alter the texture of its skin to imitate rock or coral."""),

("why can't we see stars during the day",
"""The stars are still there; they're simply outshone. During the day, sunlight scattered by the atmosphere fills the whole sky with a bright blue glow, and that glow is far brighter than the faint pinpricks of distant stars. It's the same reason you can't see a flashlight beam at noon. Get above the atmosphere and the problem vanishes: astronauts can see stars against a black sky even with the sun in view, as long as they shade their eyes from its glare."""),

("what is DNA",
"""DNA is the molecule that carries the instructions for building and running a living thing. It's a long chain made from four chemical units, usually abbreviated A, C, G, and T, arranged in an order that works like a written text. Sections of that text, called genes, spell out how to make proteins, which do most of the work in a cell. The molecule is shaped like a twisted ladder, the famous double helix, and each rung is a pair of matching units. That pairing is what lets DNA copy itself: unzip the ladder down the middle, and each half serves as a template for rebuilding the other."""),

("why does ice float",
"""Almost every substance becomes denser as it freezes, but water is an exception. As water cools toward freezing, its molecules settle into a crystal lattice held together by hydrogen bonds, and that lattice is more open, with more empty space, than liquid water. So ice is about nine percent less dense than the water it came from, and it floats. This small oddity has enormous consequences: lakes freeze from the top down, and the layer of ice insulates the water beneath it, which is why fish survive the winter."""),

("how do earthquakes happen",
"""The Earth's outer shell is broken into enormous plates that drift a few centimeters a year. Where plates meet, they often lock together at their edges while the rest keeps moving, and the rock along the boundary bends and stores strain, like a stick slowly flexed between your hands. Eventually the strain exceeds what the rock can bear, and it slips suddenly along a fault. The stored energy radiates outward as waves through the ground, and those waves are what we feel as an earthquake."""),

("what makes thunder",
"""Lightning makes thunder. A lightning bolt heats the air along its path to around thirty thousand degrees Celsius in a few millionths of a second, hotter than the surface of the sun. Air heated that fast expands explosively, sending out a shock wave that becomes the crack or rumble we hear. The sound rolls and grumbles because it arrives from different parts of a long, crooked bolt at slightly different times. Since light arrives almost instantly and sound travels about a kilometer every three seconds, you can count the gap between flash and thunder to estimate how far away the storm is."""),

("why do onions make you cry",
"""Cutting an onion breaks open its cells and sets off a small chemical defense. Enzymes released from the damaged cells convert sulfur compounds in the onion into a volatile gas that drifts upward into your eyes. When it meets the moisture there, it irritates the nerves, and your eyes flood with tears to wash it away. Chilling the onion first slows the reaction, and a very sharp knife crushes fewer cells, so both help."""),

("how does the body fight off a cold",
"""When a cold virus invades the cells lining your nose and throat, those cells release chemical alarms. Blood flow to the area increases and immune cells pour in, which is why your nose swells and runs. Some of these cells attack infected cells directly; others produce antibodies tailored to the specific virus, a process that takes several days to ramp up. Most of what makes you feel miserable, the congestion, the sore throat, the fatigue, is actually your immune response at work. Once the antibodies catch up, the infection clears, and your body keeps a memory of that particular virus, although there are hundreds of cold viruses, so the next one is usually a stranger."""),

("why are flamingos pink",
"""Because of what they eat. Flamingos are born gray, and their pink comes from pigments called carotenoids found in the algae and brine shrimp they filter out of the water. Their bodies break those pigments down and deposit them in their feathers, skin, and legs. A flamingo that stops eating carotenoid-rich food will gradually fade toward white, which is why zoos add the pigments to their birds' diets."""),

("how do tides work",
"""The moon's gravity pulls on the whole Earth, but it pulls hardest on the side facing it and weakest on the far side. That difference stretches the oceans into two bulges: one toward the moon, and one on the opposite side, where the water is, in effect, left behind. As the Earth rotates through those bulges, most coastlines pass through two high tides and two low tides a day. The sun plays a smaller part. When sun and moon line up, at new and full moon, their pulls combine and the tides run highest; when they pull at right angles, the tides are mild."""),
]
